  "overlay": [
    {
      "key": "colonoscopy_completion_12m",
      "notes": "An 'ever' figure with no stated follow-up window, used here as the 360-day target. Pre-IFHAS V10, Emirati nationals only. Inherited default 0.56 (Mohl 2023, US).",
      "source": "Almansoori et al., BMC Cancer 2021;21:897, 436 of 1,429 positives (30.5%) 'ever' had follow-up colonoscopy, Emirati nationals, Abu Dhabi ambulatory clinics, 2015-2016. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8343888/",
      "status": "sourced",
      "value": 0.305
    },
    {
      "key": "patients.refusal_prob",
      "notes": "Default 0.12 (calibrated to Mohl 2023). The audit's own anchor is gi_referral_non_attendance_abu_dhabi (0.25: about 25% of all positives referred to GI who did not attend, Almansoori 2021, derived in docs/evidence.md), which accounts for only part of the loss; the engine does not read that key. The rest of the loss the audit does not explain is also put here, as a patient-level trait in BOTH arms, so the orchestration is assumed to recover none of it. That is the conservative direction and it understates the mechanism: some of this loss (not referred, not scheduled, lost between steps) is exactly what ownership and timers act on.",
      "source": "Calibrated (E20b) so the baseline reproduces the 30.5% Abu Dhabi audit",
      "status": "calibrated",
      "value": 0.5
    },
    {
      "key": "appointment.fast_lane_share.unmanaged",
      "notes": "Default 0.84 (calibrated in B21 to Mohl 2023). Lowered so that the median time to colonoscopy among baseline completers is about 72 days, the audit's median (docs/evidence.md), and at least half of the 360-day completion is reached by 90 days. The slow-lane timing (slow_lane_lead_median_days 49, sigma) is left at its default. Managed share (orchestrated cases in reach) is untouched at 0.994.",
      "source": "Calibrated (E20b) so the baseline reproduces the 30.5% Abu Dhabi audit",
      "status": "calibrated",
      "value": 0.4
    }
  ],
